<h1 class="article-title">Cleaning Up Karosta Canal: Improving the Baltic Sea Ecosystem and Liepāja’s Environment</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>On 1 December 2021, Liepāja hosted the opening seminar for the Port of Liepāja’s Karosta Canal remediation project, “Remediation of Historically Contaminated Sites in Karosta Canal, Port of Liepāja”. The seminar, “Cleaning Up Karosta Canal: A Contribution to the Ecological Health of the Baltic Sea and Liepāja’s Environment”, highlighted the canal’s importance to Liepāja’s cultural and historical development. The cleanup will improve environmental quality and biodiversity in the canal, the Baltic Sea ecosystem and water quality, as well as local environmental conditions and residents’ health.</p><p>The project implemented by the Liepāja Special Economic Zone Authority follows remediation work carried out in 2018. Its aim is to reduce the adverse effects of historically contaminated sediments in Karosta Canal on the environment, human health and the ecological condition of the Baltic Sea.</p><p>Activities at the former military base severely affected environmental quality in Karosta Canal, making it one of Latvia’s most polluted sites. Contaminants include petroleum products (including benzene, xylene and hydrocarbons) and heavy metals (mercury, arsenic, lead, zinc, copper, selenium and cadmium), concentrated in accumulated mud and sand. Untreated municipal wastewater was also discharged into the canal before treatment facilities were built. Contamination exceeds permitted limits, degrading the ecosystem and leaving very limited flora and fauna.</p><p>Although pollutants appear to be trapped in sediments, they can affect perch living near the canal. Fish may migrate to Lake Liepāja, where perch have been found to contain markedly higher concentrations of mercury. Pollution can therefore spread beyond its original location through the food chain, affecting other levels of the ecosystem, including people.</p><p>Nevertheless, improvements have been observed in recent years. Long-term studies indicate that petroleum product concentrations in sediments have decreased almost eightfold since 1993, partly because bacteria naturally break down certain petroleum compounds. A 2018 assessment by the Latvian Environment, Geology and Meteorology Centre of pollution loads entering the Baltic Sea found that preliminary wastewater-treatment measures had been completed. It concluded that Karosta Canal remediation would improve the Baltic Sea’s ecology and water quality, potentially enabling the canal to be removed from the Baltic Marine Environment Protection Commission (HELCOM) list of pollution “hot spots”.</p><p>To improve the environment of Karosta Canal and the Baltic Sea, the remediation project will remove 202,247 m³ of sediments that do not meet regulatory standards and contain 400 tonnes of contaminants. The project is funded under the Norwegian Financial Mechanism 2014–2021 programme “Climate Change Mitigation, Adaptation and Environment”. Total project costs are EUR 5,117,647.05, including EUR 4,350,000 from the Norwegian Financial Mechanism.</p><p> </p><p>Additional information:</p><p>Between 1994 and 2010, the Liepāja SEZ Authority and government institutions funded dozens of geo-environmental studies in preparation for the canal cleanup. A decision to implement phase one was not taken until 2012. With EU Cohesion Fund support, the project “Cleanup of Historically Contaminated Sites in Karosta Canal, Port of Liepāja — Phase I” was completed by 31 December 2015. Large quantities of man-made debris, including scrap metal, concrete and timber, were removed and disposed of from 78 hectares of the canal. Approximately 60,000 m³ of polluted sediments were also dredged from the most contaminated 10-hectare section and deposited in a purpose-built disposal facility at the canal’s eastern end.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ Authority begins phase two of the Karosta Canal cleanup — https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zp1Yv1qPYi0</p><p> </p><p>Additional sources: Analysis of pollution-load assessments, “Determination of nutrient loads from pollution sources entering the Baltic Sea in order to identify effective reduction measures and sources with the greatest pollution-reduction potential”.</p><p> </p><p>Working together for a green Europe!</p></div>
